      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2017 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Posttranslational modifications (PTMs) of histones are one of the main research interests in the rapidly growing field of epigenetics. Accurate and precise quantification of these highly complex histone PTMs is critical for understanding the histone code and the biological significance behind it. It nonetheless remains a major analytical challenge. Mass spectrometry (MS) has been proven as a robust tool in retrieving quantitative information of histone PTMs, and a variety of MS-based quantitative strategies have been successfully developed and employed in basic research as well as clinical studies. In this chapter, we provide an overview for quantitative analysis of histone PTMs, often highly flexible and case dependent, as a primer for future experimental designs."
      },
